+### What categories are available?
+
+The built-in categories are **food**, **transport**, **rent**,
+**entertainment**, and **other**. You cannot invent new categories at the
+command line; add them to `KNOWN_CATEGORIES` in `tally/config.py` if you need
+something different.
+
+---
+
+## Commands
+
+### How do I see what I spent?
+
+`report` prints totals by category for a month:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03
+python -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03 --category food
+```
+
+`list` shows the individual expense lines instead of the rolled-up totals:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03
+```
+
+Both commands default to the current month if you leave off `--month`.
+
+### Does tally track budgets?
+
+Yes. Monthly budgets per category are configured in `tally/config.py`. The
+`budget` command shows how much you have spent against each budget for the
+month:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli budget --month 2024-03
+```
+
+A warning marker appears on any category that has used 80 % or more of its
+budget.
+
+### How do I get an overview across all time?
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli stats
+```
+
+This prints the total number of expenses recorded, the all-time total in USD,
+the per-expense average, and the date range they span.
+
+### Can I export my data to a spreadsheet?
+
+Yes. `export` writes everything out as CSV. Omit `--out` to print to stdout
+instead of a file:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli export --out my-expenses.csv
+```
+
+All amounts in the CSV are in US dollars.
+
+---
+
+## Data and storage
+
+### Where is my data kept?
+
+In a single JSON file on your own machine. The default location is
+`./tally.json` (next to wherever you run tally). Unless you turn on the hosted
+backup add-on, your data never leaves your computer.
+
+### Can I keep the data file somewhere else?
+
+Yes. Pass `--store` before the subcommand:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli --store ~/finances/2024.json add 12.50 food
+```
+
+You can also keep separate files for different purposes (personal vs.
+business, for example) and switch between them with `--store`.
+
+### Why does tally show my amounts in US dollars even though I typed euros?
+
+tally converts every amount to US dollars at the moment you record it, using
+the fixed conversion table in `tally/rates.py`. That converted dollar figure
+is what gets stored. Keeping everything in one currency means reports and
+totals stay consistent even when a month mixes several currencies. The
+original amount and currency you typed are saved alongside the record for
+reference, but they are never used to recompute a total.
+
+---
+
+## Currency
+
+### Can I enter expenses in other currencies?
+
+Yes. Pass `--currency` when you add an expense:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 39 food --currency EUR --date 2024-03-04
+```
+
+### Which currencies does tally support?
+
+USD, EUR, GBP, SGD, JPY, and INR. Typing an unsupported code is an error
+(rather than a silent wrong number), so a typo is caught immediately.
+
+---
+
+## Plans and backup
+
+### Is tally really free?
+
+Yes. Running tally on your own computer is free and always will be. The only
+thing that costs money is the optional hosted backup add-on.
+
+### How many expenses can I track on the free plan?
+
+There is no limit on how many expenses you record locally — tally never stops
+you from adding one. The free tier of the hosted backup add-on covers up to
+**100 expenses per month**. If you record more than that, the extra expenses
+are still saved on your machine; they simply are not included in the hosted
+backup until you upgrade to Plus or Family. Both paid plans remove the
+per-month cap.
+
+### How long does the backup keep my history?
+
+| Plan | Price | Backup history |
+|------|-------|----------------|
+| Free | $0 | 7 days |
+| Plus | $3 / month | 1 year |
+| Family | $6 / month | 3 years |
+
+### When does an upgrade or downgrade take effect?
+
+At the start of the next billing month.
+
+### Do I need the hosted backup at all?
+
+Not if you handle your own backups. The data file is a plain JSON file — you
+can copy it, version-control it, or sync it with any tool you already use. The
+hosted backup is just a convenience if you would rather not think about it.
